---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
	title="Architecture — Actual Budget Tweaks"
	description="How features, the runtime, and the Actual API bridge fit together — for anyone contributing a new feature."
>
	<main class="docs center">
		<header class="docs__header stack stack--sm">
			<a href="/" class="docs__back">&larr; Back to home</a>
			<h1>Architecture</h1>
			<p class="docs__subtitle">
				How this extension is put together, for anyone about to add a feature to it. If you're
				looking for a step-by-step walkthrough instead, see <a href="/docs/contributing">Contributing</a
				>.
			</p>
		</header>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>The shape of a feature</h2>
			<p>
				Every feature — theme, layout tweak, sidebar redesign, whatever — is one call to
				<code>defineSetting()</code>, exported as a named const, and listed in
				<code>src/features/index.ts</code>. That's the entire contract. There's no plugin manifest,
				no separate registration file, no build step beyond the normal one.
			</p>
			<pre class="docs__code"><code>{`// src/features/readability/category-progress/index.ts
export const categoryProgress = defineSetting({
  type: "checkbox",
  label: "Category Progress Bars",
  description: "Show a spending progress bar under each budget category.",
  group: "Budget",
  icon: "gauge",
  context: { key: "category-progress-indicators", defaultValue: false },
  css: () => CSS,
  init: () => {
    const unwatch = watchDom(scanAndDecorate);
    return () => {
      unwatch();
      undecorateAll();
    };
  },
});`}</code></pre>
			<p>
				<code>defineSetting()</code> itself (<code>src/features/types.ts</code>) does nothing at
				runtime — it's the identity function. Its job is entirely in the type system: it infers a
				precise literal type for whatever you pass it, which is what lets the runtime and settings UI
				work with one setting file at a time without a giant hand-written type declaration.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>The four setting types</h2>
			<div class="docs__cards">
				<div class="docs__card">
					<code class="docs__card-type">checkbox</code>
					<p>A single on/off toggle. The most common type — most tweaks are this.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="docs__card">
					<code class="docs__card-type">select</code>
					<p>A dropdown of named options (<code>options: [&#123; value, label &#125;]</code>).</p>
				</div>
				<div class="docs__card">
					<code class="docs__card-type">custom</code>
					<p>
						Renders its own Svelte component in the settings panel instead of a checkbox/select
						row — for anything with more than one control (a color picker, multiple related
						toggles). Its <code>init</code> has no cleanup return, unlike the other three — it
						manages its own lifecycle.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="docs__card">
					<code class="docs__card-type">core</code>
					<p>
						No UI card at all — always active, no user-facing toggle. Used for things like the
						release-notification check and the side-panel manager. Reserved for extension
						internals; a new feature almost never needs this type.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Runtime lifecycle</h2>
			<p>
				<code>src/features/runtime.ts</code> is the only place that calls a setting's
				<code>init</code>/cleanup. On startup, <code>bootstrapSettings()</code> reads every setting's
				persisted value and activates the ones that should be on — all concurrently
				(<code>Promise.all</code>), so one feature's slow storage read or DOM query doesn't delay
				another's CSS from applying. When a checkbox/select is toggled from the settings panel,
				<code>applySettingChange()</code> deactivates the old value and activates the new one the same
				way.
			</p>
			<p>
				Every <code>init</code>, cleanup, and bootstrap call is wrapped in its own try/catch and
				logged on failure — <strong
					>a feature that throws during activation doesn't take any other feature down with it</strong
				>, and doesn't block the rest of <code>bootstrapSettings()</code>'s <code>Promise.all</code>.
				You don't need to add your own top-level try/catch for this; the runtime already isolates
				you.
			</p>
			<p>
				What it doesn't do for you: cleanup correctness. If <code>init</code> registers a listener,
				starts an observer, or injects DOM, your returned cleanup function is the only thing that
				undoes it — nothing else runs when the setting is toggled off.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2 id="page-gating">Page gating</h2>
			<p>
				Actual is a single-page app, so a feature can't just run once at load — it has to notice
				when the user navigates to (or away from) the page it cares about. The convention here is a
				permanent <code>watchDom()</code> callback that checks <code>matchesPage()</code> internally
				and no-ops on the wrong page, <strong>not</strong> a start/stop pair triggered by route changes.
			</p>
			<pre class="docs__code"><code>{`import { watchDom } from "@lib/utilities/dom-watcher";
import { Page, matchesPage } from "@lib/utilities/pages";

function scanAndDecorate() {
  if (!matchesPage(Page.Budget)) return;
  // ...decorate whatever's currently in the DOM
}

// registered once in init(), left running for the setting's whole lifetime
const unwatch = watchDom(scanAndDecorate);`}</code></pre>
			<p>
				<code>watchDom()</code> (<code>src/lib/utilities/dom-watcher.ts</code>) is a ref-counted
				wrapper around a debounced <code>MutationObserver</code>. Callers that don't pass a
				<code>target</code> all share one observer on <code>document.body</code>; passing a
				<code>target</code> gets that node its own observer, deduped by target. Either way it's cheap
				to add another listener — you're not paying for a new observer per feature.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>The Actual API bridge</h2>
			<p>
				<code>src/lib/utilities/actual-api.ts</code> exports <code>query()</code>, <code>send()</code
				>, <code>dispatch()</code>, and <code>navigate()</code> — typed wrappers around Actual's own
				internal <code>$q</code>/<code>$query</code>/<code>$send</code> and action-creators, reached
				via a bridge injected into the page's main JS world
				(<code>src/entrypoints/actual-api-bridge-main.ts</code>).
			</p>
			<pre class="docs__code"><code>{`const cats = await query("categories");
const cell = await send("get-cell", { sheetName: "budget202506", name: "available-funds" });`}</code></pre>
			<p>
				The important thing to know before you reach for this: <strong
					>the bridge itself is generic</strong
				> — <code>query</code>/<code>send</code> forward whatever table or method name you give them.
				Typed autocomplete and return types come entirely from
				<code>ActualTable</code>/<code>SendMethodMap</code> in
				<code>src/lib/types/actual-schema.ts</code>. So if you need a table or internal method that
				isn't typed yet, you almost never need to touch the runtime bridge — add the type to
				<code>actual-schema.ts</code> and it works.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Storage</h2>
			<p>
				<code>src/lib/utilities/store.ts</code> exports <code>getValue</code>/<code>setValue</code>/<code
					>hasValue</code
				>, thin wrappers around <code>browser.storage.local</code> with a <code>local:</code> key
				prefix. A setting's own persisted value (<code>context.key</code>) is handled for you by the
				runtime — you'd reach for these directly only for extra state a feature needs beyond its
				single on/off value (category icon assignments, a cached API response, and so on).
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Registering a feature</h2>
			<p>
				<code>pnpm new-feature</code> does this step for you (see <a href="/docs/contributing"
					>Contributing</a
				>). If you're doing it by hand: add the import to <code>src/features/index.ts</code>, then
				add the identifier to the category array it belongs in (<code>readability</code>,
				<code>appearance</code>, <code>layoutAndDensity</code>, <code>workflows</code>). That
				array is also what <code>scriptSections</code> renders in the settings panel and what
				<code>scripts/generate-features-manifest.mjs</code> reads to build the public
				<a href="/features">features list</a> — one array, both places.
			</p>
			<p>
				That manifest script parses <code>index.ts</code> as a TypeScript AST rather than importing
				it, so it doesn't have to pull Svelte/browser dependencies into a plain Node script. One
				consequence: <code>label</code>/<code>description</code>/<code>icon</code>/<code>group</code>
				 must be plain string literals in the <code>defineSetting()</code> call, not computed
				expressions — a non-literal value is read back empty with no error, and the feature quietly
				drops off the public features list.
			</p>
		</section>
	</main>
</Base>

<style>
	.docs {
		padding-block: 3rem 5rem;
		max-inline-size: 760px;
	}

	.docs__header h1 {
		font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.4rem);
		font-weight: 700;
		letter-spacing: -0.03em;
	}

	.docs__subtitle {
		color: var(--text-secondary);
		font-size: 1rem;
		max-inline-size: 60ch;
	}

	.docs__back {
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		color: var(--text-secondary);
		text-decoration: none;
	}

	.docs__back:hover {
		color: var(--accent);
	}

	.docs__section {
		margin-block-start: 3rem;
	}

	.docs__section h2 {
		font-size: 1.3rem;
		font-weight: 600;
		letter-spacing: -0.02em;
		margin-block-end: 0.9rem;
	}

	.docs__section p {
		font-size: 0.92rem;
		line-height: 1.65;
		color: var(--text-secondary);
	}

	.docs__section p + p {
		margin-block-start: 0.9rem;
	}

	.docs__section code {
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: 0.82em;
		background: var(--accent-wash);
		color: var(--accent);
		padding: 0.1rem 0.35rem;
		border-radius: 4px;
	}

	.docs__section strong {
		color: var(--text);
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	.docs__code {
		margin-block: 1rem;
		padding: 1rem 1.1rem;
		background: var(--dark-bg);
		border: 1px solid var(--dark-border);
		border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
		overflow-x: auto;
	}

	.docs__code code {
		background: none;
		color: var(--dark-text-light);
		padding: 0;
		font-size: 0.8rem;
		line-height: 1.6;
		white-space: pre;
	}

	.docs__cards {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
		gap: 0.9rem;
	}

	.docs__card {
		padding: 1rem 1.1rem;
		background: var(--surface);
		border: 1px solid var(--border);
		border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
	}

	.docs__card-type {
		display: inline-block;
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: 0.78rem;
		background: var(--accent-wash);
		color: var(--accent);
		padding: 0.15rem 0.5rem;
		border-radius: 4px;
		margin-block-end: 0.5rem;
	}

	.docs__card p {
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		line-height: 1.55;
		color: var(--text-secondary);
	}

	.docs__card code {
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: 0.85em;
		background: var(--accent-wash);
		color: var(--accent);
		padding: 0.05rem 0.3rem;
		border-radius: 3px;
	}

	@media (max-width: 640px) {
		.docs__cards {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
		}
	}
</style>
